 that  regard uh  classification system like classifying  animals by their species  is a more refined way of classification  uh compared to clustering but really we  are talking about  uh grouping subgroups  based on similarities that they have  so how can we get started with this  computational classification i would say  that we are so interested in  first i would say it is really really  important to  identify a problem as i am going to tell  you the problem that i work with  is cancer particularly neuroblastoma  which is a  pediatric cancer  the other thing that i would say is  important is to identify  a sponsor so a mentor a school  or a group a science group that you  might be interested in  and uh really the  what you need is someone or an  institution that has the potential  to guide you through the process and  frankly  i joined omixlogic a couple of weeks ago  i'm still  getting used to all the resources that  they have but  so far i think the resources that i have  seen and have  tested and use it they they're very very  available resources in the  bioinformatics field  so i said that it's important to  identify a problem that you're  interested in and the one that i  told you i am interested in is  neuroblastoma it's a complex  or polygenic disease i'll not go  too much into details but i want to say  that it's  a problem in the development of the  sympathetic  nervous system so the sympathetic  nervous system is this part of the  nervous system that connects organs to  the central nervous system here  in the spine for instance as you can see  the other problem that i want to go to  in neuroblastoma  is this concept that we have two major  populations in the tumor so we have  the whole mass of the tumor  there we could argue that there are many  subpopulations but  i am going to talk tell you about the  two main subgroups the two major  populations which are the  population of adrenergic cells that look  actually like neurons that's why you see  these p